# What this ACL can and cannot enforce — read this before trusting it.
#
# Topics are  ec/<room>/<sender-client-id>  where <room> is a secret only the
# two members can derive. The intent was that knowing the room = the capability
# to be in it, exactly as GossipSub gives for free.
#
# A static broker ACL CANNOT deliver that. To receive its room a member must be
# granted read on ec/<room>/+, and since <room> is a runtime secret the only
# static grant that covers it is ec/+/+ — which grants read on EVERY room to
# EVERYONE. Verified against the live broker (2026-07-31): a client subscribing
# to `#` receives every room's traffic; only $SYS is blocked (it is outside the
# grant). Content stays end-to-end encrypted, so what leaks is METADATA — which
# rooms are active (i.e. who is talking to whom), and message timing and size.
#
# This is a genuine weakening versus GossipSub, where topics are unguessable and
# there is no wildcard subscribe. DO NOT enable the MQTT transport where
# cross-room metadata visible to any connected client is unacceptable. True
# isolation needs a broker auth plugin that treats the room secret as a
# subscribe capability (see README, "MQTT metadata caveat").

# Write is scoped to the client's own id: a member can publish only as itself
# (ec/<room>/<ownid>), so it cannot forge another sender inside a room. Weak —
# the id is client-chosen — but it stops accidental and casual impersonation.
pattern write ec/+/%c

# Read is necessarily broad (see above). This is the leak; it cannot be narrowed
# without per-connection capability checks the static ACL does not have.
topic read ec/+/+

# The broker's own telemetry is denied outright — the one thing the ACL does buy.
topic deny $SYS/#
